course: world
lesson: 74
title: Fish, frogs and reptiles
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A fish takes in water through its mouth and pushes it out over its gills, where the blood picks up the oxygen dissolved in the water. Its tail fin drives it forward, and its other fins steer and keep it upright.
explain: Many fish have a swim bladder, a bag of gas inside the body; by changing how much gas is in it, the fish can float at any depth without sinking or rising. A line of special cells along each side, the lateral line, feels the smallest movements in the water.
explain: Frogs and other amphibians have thin, moist skin, and they can take in some oxygen through it, so they must keep it damp; their eggs have no shell and dry out in air, so they are laid in water or in damp places.
explain: Reptiles have dry, scaly skin that keeps water in, and their eggs have a tough shell, so they can live far from water, even in deserts.
explain: Fish, amphibians and reptiles get their warmth from their surroundings. For example, a lizard basks on a sunny rock in the morning to warm up and hides in the shade at noon, and because it does not burn food for heat, it may need only a tiny part of the food a mouse of the same size eats.
fact: Fish take oxygen from the water with their gills.
fact: A fish's tail fin pushes it forward, and its other fins steer and balance it.
fact: Many fish have a swim bladder filled with gas that lets them float at one depth.
fact: The lateral line along a fish's side feels movements in the water.
fact: A shark's skeleton is made of cartilage, the bendy material in our ears and nose, not bone.
fact: A shark has no swim bladder, so it keeps swimming to stay up, and its light, bendy skeleton helps it.
fact: A frog can take in some oxygen through its thin, moist skin.
fact: Because their skin is thin, frogs are easily harmed by polluted water.
fact: Many frogs catch insects with a long, sticky tongue.
fact: Amphibian eggs have no shell, so they are laid in water or damp places.
fact: Reptiles' dry, scaly skin keeps water in their bodies, so many can live in deserts.
fact: A snake flicks its tongue to pick up smells from the air.
fact: Snakes shed their skin as they grow, often in one piece.
fact: Animals that get their warmth from their surroundings need much less food than mammals and birds of the same size.
exercise: How does a fish get oxygen? | Its gills take oxygen dissolved in the water | It swallows bubbles of air when it swims up to the surface | It breaks water into oxygen in its stomach | It takes in air through its scales
exercise: What does a fish's tail fin do? | Pushes the fish forward | Helps it breathe under water | Helps it smell food far away | Keeps it warm in cold water
exercise: What is a swim bladder for? | Letting a fish float at the depth it chooses | Storing food inside it for the long cold winter | Breathing air like lungs | Laying eggs in safe places
exercise: What does the lateral line of a fish do? | It feels movements in the water | It shows how old the fish is | It helps the fish taste its food | It makes the fish's scales grow
exercise: What is a shark's skeleton made of? | Cartilage, like our ears and nose | Bone, like ours | Hard shell, like a crab's or a lobster's | It has no skeleton at all
exercise: Why must a frog keep its skin damp? | It takes in some oxygen through its skin | Dry skin would make it too heavy to jump | Its skin would change colour and it would be seen | Wet skin helps it to see better under water
exercise: Why are frogs easily harmed by polluted water? | Their thin skin lets harmful things in easily | They drink much more water than fish, so they swallow more dirt | They have no gills or lungs at all | Pollution makes the water too cold
exercise: How do many frogs catch insects? | With a long, sticky tongue | With sharp claws | With a poison sting | With strong jaws and teeth
exercise: Why are frogs' eggs laid in water or damp places? | They have no shell and would dry out | They need to be cold to hatch | Water makes them invisible | The eggs are too heavy to lie on land
exercise: Why can many reptiles live in deserts? | Their dry, scaly skin keeps water in | They drink sea water | They never get hot | They can store rain in their tails
exercise: Why does a snake flick its tongue? | To pick up smells from the air | To cool its body down on hot days | To frighten away all of its enemies | To taste the ground for water to drink
exercise: What does a snake do as it grows? | Sheds its skin, often in one piece | Grows extra legs | Changes into a lizard when it grows up | Loses its scales forever
exercise: Why does a lizard lie in the sun in the morning? | To warm its body up from the outside | To dry its eggs | To make its skin darker for hunting | To breathe more easily
exercise: Why does a lizard eat much less than a mouse of the same size? | It does not burn food to keep itself warm | It is too lazy to hunt | Its food is much bigger | It sleeps all day and all night, so it never gets hungry
test: A fish stays still in the middle of a tank, neither sinking nor rising. What helps it do this? | Its swim bladder | Its lateral line | Its tail fin | Its gills
test: A fish in a dark, muddy river swims away before a pike, a big hunting fish, can reach it. How did it sense the pike? | Its lateral line felt the water move | It heard the pike's heartbeat through the water | It smelled the pike's breath as it came closer | It saw the pike clearly even in the dark water
test: Scientists find fewer frogs in a pond each year. What might this warn them about? | The water may be polluted | The pond is getting too clean | There are too many insects | The frogs have moved into the sea
test: Why could a frog live in a desert much less easily than a lizard? | Its thin, moist skin would dry out | It needs more sunshine than a lizard | Its eggs need hot sand | It cannot eat insects in a desert
test: A pet snake has dull, cloudy eyes and soon leaves behind a long, thin, empty skin. What happened? | It shed its old skin as it grew | It was sick and lost its scales | Another snake lost its skin there | It changed into a new kind of snake
test: A zoo feeds its crocodile much less often than its lion. Why is this possible? | The crocodile does not burn food to keep warm | The crocodile eats only plants | The lion is much smaller than the crocodile | The crocodile drinks more water
test: Which is true about a shark? | It is a fish whose skeleton is cartilage, not bone | It is a mammal that breathes air, like a whale or a dolphin | It is a reptile with fins | It has no skeleton at all
test: A frog sits still and an insect flies near. What happens next? | The frog shoots out its long, sticky tongue | The frog chases it with its claws | The frog stings it with poison | The frog waits for it to land in its mouth
test: A turtle lays its eggs on a dry beach, but a frog lays its eggs in a pond. What is the main reason? | The turtle's eggs have a tough shell that keeps water in | Frogs cannot walk onto beaches | The turtle wants its eggs to be cold | Pond water is too warm for turtle eggs
test: why's the frog pond gone quiet since the factory, frogs gone? | Frogs' thin skin is easily harmed by polluted water | Frogs only leave because the water got too cold for them | Factory noise frightens the frogs, so they move to the sea | Frogs need oily water to keep their thin skin wet and soft
test: how do sharks float with no swim bladder thingy | They keep swimming, and their skeleton is light bendy cartilage | They fill their bones with air like birds | They hold their breath at the surface | They sleep on the sea floor and never float
test: Leo says snakes flick their tongue to scare u. true? | No, they flick it to pick up smells from the air | Yes, it is only a warning to stay back and keep away | Yes, and they also use it to taste the ground | No, they flick it to cool their bodies down
test: my brother reckons fish breathe air with lungs like us. right? | No, fish take oxygen from the water with their gills | Yes, fish swim up to breathe air every minute | Yes, but only big fish like sharks | No, fish do not need any oxygen at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which fish obtain oxygen? | They take oxygen from the water with their gills | They breathe air at the surface with lungs | They take oxygen through their scales alone | They obtain oxygen from the food they eat
exam: Identify the function of the swim bladder of a fish. | It permits the fish to float at one depth | It enables the fish to digest its food | It permits the fish to sense movements in the water | It enables the fish to breathe air
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which frogs are readily harmed by polluted water. | Their skin is thin and moist | Their skin is dry and scaly | They possess no gills at any stage | They drink great quantities of water
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the skeleton of a shark. | It is composed of cartilage, not bone | It is composed of solid bone throughout | It is composed of hollow bone | A shark possesses no skeleton whatsoever
